<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dimasa people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dimasa Kacharis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dimasa, also called Dimasa Kacharis, are a Tibeto-Burman people of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, living mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dima-hasao">Dima Hasao</a>, Karbi Anglong and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cachar">Cachar</a> districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagaland">Nagaland</a>. They speak Dimasa, a Boro-Garo language, and are organised in clans. Their kings ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimasa-kingdom">Kachari kingdom</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimapur">Dimapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maibang">Maibang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaspur">Khaspur</a> until 1832.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dimasa-people.jpg" alt="Dimasa people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khdibyajyoti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barman_Dimasa_Girl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
